cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you reference this formalization, please cite it as below."
title: "Gödel's Loophole, kernel-checked"
type: software
abstract: >-
  A toy constitutional state machine in core Lean 4, after the argument
  Kurt Gödel reportedly began at his 1947 U.S. citizenship hearing and
  never wrote down. Models a constitution as a formal system whose rule
  of change is itself an object-level provision, and proves in seven
  movements that legal reachability of dictatorship is a theorem: the
  amendment rule does not entrench itself (A); an entrenchment clause
  that does not protect itself is a two-step no-op (B); a self-entrenched
  eternity clause is formally sound (C); endogenizing the validity
  predicate defeats it while leaving the clause in the text (D); a
  ratchet on the amendment threshold holds as the modeller's fiat but
  guards nothing else, falls in three steps when written as a
  provision, and self-entrenched holds by exactly C's proof, no new
  kind of defence (E); on-chain, code-is-law is a
  theorem, yet the fork goes through at bare majority and the canonical
  post-fork history is code-invalid (F); and, as a schema over every
  machine, any configuration is one repoint from being in force (G).
  56 theorems, no mathlib, no sorry, zero axioms on the pinned
  toolchain. No formal reduction of Gödel's second incompleteness
  theorem to the constitutional case is claimed.
